1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 { The so-called anti-Berlin, which was long considered completely harmless but has been gaining more and more popularity in recent years due to futility of White's attempts in the Berlin Endgame. } Bc5 5. c3 ( { A starting point of this chapter. This is considered a more accurate move than } 5. O-O { , because it prevents ...Nd4. However, it gives Black an attractive possibility of an immediate strike in the centre. } ) 
d5 $1 ( { Of course } 5... O-O { and } d6 { are viable alternatives, but the text is the most active and direct way of solving Black's opening problems. I offer a similar approach of aiming at ...d5 instead of ...d6 against the Giuoco Piano. } ) 
6. Nbd2 dxe4 { Black could do without this move for a while, but that would only give White an additional possibility of exd5, Bc4, b3, Ne4 etc. } 7. dxe4 O-O 8. O-O Qe7 9. Qe2 { -KEY- } a5 { It's not like we were particularly afraid of b4 but if White didn't play let's just stop it and improve our position on the queenside. } 10. a4 Nb8 $1 { The key maneuver to remember. It originates from correspondence chess and was used over the board by Grischuk against Navara and Ivanchuk in 2017. Black achieves comfortable play by rerouting the knight to the more desirable d7 square. } 11. Nb3 Bd6 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bh4 Nbd7 ( { There is no need to play } 13... g5 { , giving White a tempting possibility of } 14. Nxg5 $5 { } ( { It's just more natural to develop the knight first. } 13... Rd8 { can even run into } 14. Nxe5 { intending f4 and e5 . } ) 
 ) 
14. h3 Rd8 { The rook occupies the only open file, but most importantly vacates the f8-square for the ...Nf8-g6 maneuver. } 15. Rfd1 Nf8 16. Bg3 Be6 ( { It is important not to fall into the } 16... Nh5 $2 17. Bxe5 $1 { trap. } ) 
17. Nbd2 Nh5 18. Bh2 $6 { This was dubious. } Ng6 $15 { Black already took over the initiative in Navara-Grischuk, EU-chT 2017 } { _ } 
