[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 { The so-called anti-Berlin, which was long considered completely harmless but has been gaining more and more popularity in recent years due to the futility of White's attempts in the Berlin Endgame. } Bc5 5. c3 ( { A starting point of this chapter. This is considered a more accurate move than } 5. O-O { , because it prevents ...Nd4. However, it gives Black an attractive possibility of an immediate strike in the centre. } ) 
d5 $1 ( { Of course } 5... O-O { and } d6 { are viable alternatives, but the text is the most active and direct way of solving Black's opening problems. I offer a similar approach of aiming at ... d5 instead of ...d6 against the Giuoco Piano. } ) 
6. exd5 { -KEY- } Qxd5 7. Bc4 { A must, as Bxf2 was threatened. } Qd8 $5 ( 7... Qd6 { has been played almost exclusively, but I find the text move much more practical, as Black does not have to worry about all the potential attacks against the queen (Nbd2-e4/c4, b4, a4, Ba3, etc.) and can continue with harmonious development. } ) 
8. Bg5 { This move has been recommended by Nils Grandelius on Chessable. It has to be admitted that it is a serious attempt for an advantage.' } h6 9. Bh4 $6 { This is inaccurate, but it was played in a high-level encounter Shankland - Mamedyarov, Saint Louis 2021. } g5 $1 { A very concrete move after which Black is taking over the initiative. } 10. Bg3 e4 { That's it, we are putting White under severe pressure. } 11. Qe2 Qe7 12. dxe4 Nxe4 { Black is about to take on g3 gaining two bishops with a tiny edge. } 
