1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 { The so-called anti-Berlin, which was long considered completely harmless but has been gaining more and more popularity in recent years due to the futility of White's attempts in the Berlin Endgame. } Bc5 5. c3 ( { A starting point of this chapter. This is considered a more accurate move than } 5. O-O { , because it prevents ...Nd4. However, it gives Black an attractive possibility of an immediate strike in the centre. } ) 
d5 $1 ( { Of course } 5... O-O { and } d6 { are viable alternatives, but the text is the most active and direct way of solving Black's opening problems. I offer a similar approach of aiming at ... d5 instead of ...d6 against the Giuoco Piano. } ) 
6. exd5 Qxd5 7. Bc4 { A must, as Bxf2 was threatened. } Qd8 $5 ( 7... Qd6 { has been played almost exclusively, but I find the text move much more practical, as Black does not have to worry about all the potential attacks against the queen (Nbd2-e4/c4, b4, a4, Ba3, etc.) and can continue with harmonious development. } ) 
8. Bg5 { This move has been recommended by Nils Grandelius on Chessable. It has to be admitted that it is a serious attempt for an advantage.' } h6 9. Bxf6 { -KEY- } Qxf6 10. Nbd2 { White exchanged his bishop for our knight arguing that now Black's queen is misplaced and he can easily push pawns on the queenside while we don't have any targets. Indeed White has nice control and our bishops aren't too threatening at the moment, but there is a nice plan for us. } Bb6 $1 ( { Not mentioned by Grandelius. Black steps out of the Ne4-Nxc5 idea and prepares ...Bf5 and ...O-O-O. He considers mainly } 10... O-O { and after } 11. O-O Bb6 12. Re1 Bg4 13. h3 Bh5 14. Bd5 { it's not much, but White is slightly better. } ) 
11. O-O Bf5 12. Ne4 ( 12. Re1 O-O-O { is fine too. } ) 
Qe7 13. b4 { A logical queenside expansion, which should not, however, discourage Black from castling the long way. } O-O-O 14. Qe2 Rhe8 { Reinforcing the centre. If White waits with a4 now, the plan of ...Kb8, ...Bc8 and ...f5 should give Black adequate counterplay. } 15. a4 a5 16. Rfe1 f6 $11 { Just reinforcing the e5-point again. White has no clear way to proceed on the queenside, e.g. } 17. Bb5 Bd7 18. bxa5 Nxa5 19. Rab1 Kb8 $11 { Black's king is a bit more exposed, but he has a better structure. } 
